<h2>New patient acquisition: turning clicks into appointments</h2>

<p>Attracting new patients is where many practices spend the most money and see the least return. You might be running Google Ads, posting on social media, or relying on word of mouth. But the real question is: what happens when someone actually shows interest?</p>

<h3>Website forms and landing pages</h3>

<p>When a potential patient finds your practice online, they need a clear and simple way to get in touch. A dedicated landing page for new patients, with a short form that captures name, phone number, email, and the reason for their visit, removes friction from the process. The moment that form is submitted, your system should send an instant confirmation message and notify your front desk.</p>

<p>Speed matters enormously here. Research consistently shows that responding to an enquiry within five minutes dramatically increases the chance of booking. If your team is busy with patients in the chair, automation handles that first touchpoint so no lead goes cold.</p>

<h3>Google Ads and local search</h3>

<p>For practices running Google Ads, the landing page is everything. Sending ad traffic to your homepage is a common mistake. Instead, build a focused page that matches the search intent. If someone searches "emergency dentist near me," they should land on a page about emergency services with a prominent booking form, not your general practice overview.</p>

<p>Your CRM tracks where each lead came from, so you can see exactly which campaigns are filling chairs and which are burning budget. This kind of visibility turns <strong>dental marketing</strong> from guesswork into a measurable system.</p>

<h3>Nurturing enquiries that do not book immediately</h3>

<p>Not every new patient enquiry converts on the first visit to your website. Some people are comparing practices, checking reviews, or simply not ready yet. A <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">structured follow-up system</a> keeps your practice top of mind without being pushy. A sequence of two or three emails over the following week, sharing useful information about your practice, your team, and what a first visit looks like, can turn a hesitant browser into a booked patient.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Review generation: building trust on autopilot</h2>

<p>Online reviews are one of the most powerful tools in <strong>dental marketing</strong>. When a new patient searches for a dentist, they almost always check Google reviews before making a decision. Practices with a steady stream of recent, positive reviews consistently outperform those with a handful of ageing testimonials.</p>

<p>The challenge is that happy patients rarely leave reviews on their own. They need a gentle nudge at the right moment. The ideal time to ask is shortly after an appointment, when the experience is still fresh and the patient is feeling good about their visit.</p>

<p>An automated review request sent via text message one to two hours after the appointment, with a direct link to your Google Business Profile, makes it effortless for patients to share their experience. We have written a detailed guide on <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">how to get more Google reviews for your local business</a> that covers the full strategy, including how to handle negative feedback gracefully.</p>

<blockquote><p>The practices that collect the most reviews are not the ones with the best dentistry. They are the ones that make it easiest for patients to leave feedback. A single text message with a direct link outperforms any amount of "please leave us a review" signage in the waiting room.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Treatment plan follow-ups: recovering unscheduled revenue</h2>

<p>Every dental practice has a list of patients who were presented with a treatment plan and never booked the work. Crowns, implants, orthodontic consultations, cosmetic procedures. These represent significant revenue sitting in your patient management system, waiting to be recovered.</p>

<p>Manual follow-up on unscheduled treatment is time-consuming and often feels awkward for staff. Automation removes both problems. When a treatment plan is created but not scheduled within a set period, the system can send a thoughtful reminder that addresses common hesitations:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Reassurance about the procedure and what to expect</li>
  <li>Information about payment plans or financing options</li>
  <li>A clear call to action to book or to call with questions</li>
</ul>

<p>These messages should feel caring, not salesy. The goal is to help patients follow through on treatment they have already agreed they need. A well-timed <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email sequence</a> can recover thousands of dollars in unscheduled treatment each month.</p>

<h3>Timing the follow-up</h3>

<p>The sweet spot for treatment plan reminders varies by procedure. A simple filling might warrant a follow-up after one week. A larger case like implants or orthodontics might benefit from a longer nurture sequence that addresses cost concerns, shares patient stories, and answers frequently asked questions over several weeks.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Insurance reminders: helping patients use their benefits</h2>

<p>One of the most effective and underused <strong>dental practice automation</strong> strategies is reminding patients about their insurance benefits before they expire. Most dental insurance plans operate on a calendar year, and unused benefits do not roll over. Patients who have not used their annual maximum are essentially leaving money on the table.</p>

<p>An automated campaign in October or November that reminds patients of their remaining benefits creates urgency that is genuinely helpful. The message is simple: "You still have dental benefits available this year. If you do not use them by 31 December, they reset to zero. Book now to make the most of your coverage."</p>

<p>This approach works because it is honest and patient-focused. You are not manufacturing urgency. You are helping people get the care they have already paid for through their insurance premiums. It also happens to fill your chairs during what might otherwise be a quieter period.</p>

<h3>Segmenting by insurance type</h3>

<p>If your <strong>dentist CRM</strong> tracks insurance information, you can tailor these reminders based on the patient's specific plan. Patients with plans that renew mid-year get their reminder at the appropriate time. Patients without insurance can receive a different message highlighting your practice's payment options or membership plans instead.</p>

<h2>Patient communication preferences and privacy</h2>

<p>Dental practices handle sensitive health information, and patients rightly expect their data to be treated with care. While Australian practices operate under the Privacy Act rather than HIPAA, the principles are similar: patient information must be stored securely, shared appropriately, and communicated through channels the patient has consented to.</p>

<p>When setting up your <strong>dental practice automation</strong>, keep these considerations front of mind:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Let patients choose their channel.</strong> Some patients prefer text messages. Others want email. A few still prefer phone calls. Your system should respect these preferences and communicate accordingly.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep messages general.</strong> Automated reminders should avoid including specific treatment details. "It is time for your next appointment" is appropriate. "Your root canal follow-up is due" in a text message is not.</li>
  <li><strong>Make opting out easy.</strong> Every automated message should include a clear way for patients to unsubscribe or adjust their preferences. This is both a legal requirement and good practice.</li>
  <li><strong>Store consent records.</strong> Your CRM should log when and how each patient consented to receive communications. This protects your practice and builds trust.</li>
</ul>

<p>For more on balancing effective communication with patient respect, our guide on <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS marketing for small business</a> covers the principles of text-based outreach that apply directly to dental practices.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best patient communication systems give people control over how and when they hear from you. When patients feel respected, they stay loyal. When they feel spammed, they find another dentist.</p></blockquote>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>Dental practices that invest in automation are not replacing the human touch. They are freeing up their team to focus on the work that actually requires a human: chairside care, complex conversations, and building genuine relationships with patients. The repetitive, time-sensitive tasks (recall reminders, review requests, treatment follow-ups, insurance notifications) are exactly the kind of work that software handles better than people.</p>

<p>The practices seeing the best results are the ones that connect all these systems together. When your recall reminders, new patient workflows, review generation, and treatment follow-ups all live in one platform, you get a complete picture of every patient's journey. You can see who is overdue, who has unscheduled treatment, who left a review, and who has not been in for a year. That visibility is what turns a busy practice into a growing one.</p>

<p>Start with the system that will have the biggest impact for your practice. For most, that is recall reminders. Once that is running smoothly, layer in review generation, then treatment follow-ups, then insurance campaigns. Each piece builds on the last, and before long, your practice is running a marketing engine that works while you focus on your patients.</p>

